WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.166 --> 00:00:06.500
It's really hard to tell
the story coherently

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.625
of what happens to plastic.

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:11.500
To watch the news and to look
at all the media coverage,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.666
you would think the
debate about plastic waste

00:00:13.666 --> 00:00:15.291
has already been resolved and won.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.583
But then if you actually
look at the numbers,

00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:24.333
not only has the amount of
plastic that we're producing

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:26.333
massively increased,
it's gonna increase again

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:28.166
significantly in the next 10 years.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.333
We are producing more plastic than ever

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.958
and it has to go somewhere,
so it's very important to know

00:00:35.958 --> 00:00:38.625
what happens to this stuff
after we finished using it.

00:00:40.166 --> 00:00:43.250
Tesco is a very large supermarket chain,

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.041
it's the U.K.'s largest by far.

00:00:45.041 --> 00:00:47.500
It makes an enormous amount
of profit every year.

00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:52.125
I noticed at my local Tesco Superstore

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:54.833
that these white boxes have appeared

00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:57.916
with a big sign on saying,
recycle your soft plastics here,

00:00:57.916 --> 00:00:59.708
recycle your wrappers, your bags,

00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:02.875
your crisp packets, your bread bags.

00:01:02.875 --> 00:01:05.000
I wanted to find an electronic
way to follow this stuff

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.208
so I could be absolutely sure,

00:01:06.208 --> 00:01:08.083
because it's very difficult

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:10.333
to track waste streams around the world.

00:01:12.208 --> 00:01:14.875
So, we've just seen a red unmarked lorry

00:01:14.875 --> 00:01:18.708
and we're gonna follow it for
a while and see where it goes.

00:01:19.916 --> 00:01:24.791
I assumed that all the
waste collected by Tesco

00:01:24.791 --> 00:01:25.708
would have a single outcome.

00:01:25.708 --> 00:01:26.583
It would go to one place.

00:01:26.583 --> 00:01:28.708
It would either be buried
or it would be recycled,

00:01:28.708 --> 00:01:29.916
but what I discovered was a much more

00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:31.583
complicated picture than that.

00:01:32.666 --> 00:01:35.333
It just is very complicated, very messy.

00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:36.416
There's good and bad.

00:01:44.625 --> 00:01:45.666
Hi, my name's Kit,

00:01:45.666 --> 00:01:47.958
I'm an investigative reporter with Bloomberg.

00:01:47.958 --> 00:01:52.000
Most people have no idea
what happens to their waste

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.458
once they put it in a bin or a trash can

00:01:55.458 --> 00:01:57.333
and that's especially true of plastic.

00:01:58.791 --> 00:02:00.208
It just goes away.

00:02:00.208 --> 00:02:01.208
It's collected by a van

00:02:01.208 --> 00:02:03.208
or it's deposited at a recycling point

00:02:03.208 --> 00:02:05.250
and then it goes into
this hidden ecosystem

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.333
that very few people have oversight of.

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.583
It goes off to sort of dirty,
smelly places to be sorted

00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:12.125
and dealt with by someone else

00:02:12.125 --> 00:02:13.916
and you don't think about it again.

00:02:13.916 --> 00:02:16.583
But I was fascinated to find
out exactly what happens to it

00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:19.208
and exactly where these
places are and where it goes.

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:25.375
Tesco makes a big play of
reuse as one of its efforts

00:02:25.375 --> 00:02:28.208
to help prevent plastic pollution.

00:02:28.208 --> 00:02:29.375
And by that they mean

00:02:29.375 --> 00:02:31.958
that the plastic products
they're selling to consumers

00:02:31.958 --> 00:02:33.208
they want to be able to recycle them

00:02:33.208 --> 00:02:35.833
and turn them into something
else of equivalent.

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:38.291
So they want to be able to reuse bottles

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:39.750
and packaging that they sell.

00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:45.208
I had three digital trackers
placed in wrappers or bags

00:02:45.208 --> 00:02:46.708
and put into the Tesco boxes,

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:48.500
which said they were
going to recycle them.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.333
And then I had to wait a few
days to see where they went.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.625
And yeah, they started to
move within three or four days

00:02:56.625 --> 00:02:58.166
of me dropping them off

00:02:58.166 --> 00:03:00.625
and I could see them moving by truck.

00:03:00.625 --> 00:03:04.375
One of them went to East London
and then sort of disappeared

00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:07.083
into what looked like
the banks of the Thames.

00:03:07.083 --> 00:03:09.250
It may have been lost, it
may have been shaken loose.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.916
I really don't know what happened to it.

00:03:10.916 --> 00:03:13.541
But the two other ones
took a pretty clear path,

00:03:13.541 --> 00:03:16.833
which was to go to these
Tesco logistics centers

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.791
on the outskirts of London,

00:03:19.791 --> 00:03:22.166
and then they both
headed for the same port,

00:03:22.166 --> 00:03:24.000
which is Harwich International.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.416
And from there they went
quiet for a few hours

00:03:27.416 --> 00:03:28.500
and the next time I saw them

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.250
was when they were in the
Netherlands and heading East.

00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:35.666
There are some kinds of plastic

00:03:35.666 --> 00:03:37.666
that are relatively easy to recycle

00:03:37.666 --> 00:03:40.375
and those can be sold on
the open market profitably.

00:03:40.375 --> 00:03:42.708
Things like clear plastic bottles

00:03:42.708 --> 00:03:44.250
are made from something called PET,

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.625
and that's good recycling material.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.583
You can reuse that over and over again.

00:03:47.583 --> 00:03:50.208
Clear plastic wrap, as long
as it doesn't have a label

00:03:50.208 --> 00:03:53.125
or colorants or heat retardants in it

00:03:53.125 --> 00:03:54.541
is good recycling material.

00:03:54.541 --> 00:03:57.333
You can melt that down and reprocess it.

00:03:57.333 --> 00:03:59.375
But as anyone who goes to the supermarket

00:03:59.375 --> 00:04:02.791
to buy food will know, hardly
any of the plastic products

00:04:02.791 --> 00:04:05.291
that we're sold are just clear wrap.

00:04:05.291 --> 00:04:07.291
They have labels on, they will normally be

00:04:07.291 --> 00:04:09.000
coming into contact with food.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.416
And all that means that
its not all the same stuff.

00:04:12.416 --> 00:04:13.833
It's different grades of plastic.

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:16.583
And you can't just put all
that material together into one

00:04:16.583 --> 00:04:18.875
bulk load, and then melt and recycle it,

00:04:18.875 --> 00:04:20.416
what you'd get if you did that is

00:04:20.416 --> 00:04:22.875
unusable, terrible quality plastic.

00:04:22.875 --> 00:04:24.958
So what you need to is separate it.

00:04:24.958 --> 00:04:27.166
And to this day, the only way to do that

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.541
is to have people go through by hand,

00:04:29.541 --> 00:04:31.000
feel the material with their fingers.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.625
Sometimes burn it with a lighter

00:04:32.625 --> 00:04:34.416
to work out what grade of plastic it is

00:04:34.416 --> 00:04:36.375
and separate it into different streams.

00:04:37.625 --> 00:04:40.833
I had no idea that there's
this incredibly expensive

00:04:40.833 --> 00:04:43.125
and time-consuming process
that has to go into

00:04:43.125 --> 00:04:44.916
separating all the different grades.

00:04:47.916 --> 00:04:49.333
They didn't stay long in the Netherlands.

00:04:49.333 --> 00:04:50.625
They passed through very quickly

00:04:50.625 --> 00:04:54.666
and then they went on a German motorway

00:04:54.666 --> 00:04:56.083
and zipped across the country

00:04:56.083 --> 00:04:58.416
in the space of 12 to 24 hours.

00:05:00.208 --> 00:05:02.208
Finally they crossed
the border into Poland

00:05:02.208 --> 00:05:04.291
and I could see that
both digital trackers,

00:05:04.291 --> 00:05:05.833
although this happened on different days,

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:08.666
ended up at precisely the same spot.

00:05:08.666 --> 00:05:12.916
This is the Eurokey site in Zielona Gora.

00:05:12.916 --> 00:05:16.125
Zielona Gora is a small
town in west Poland.

00:05:16.125 --> 00:05:18.666
It's relatively close to
the border with Germany.

00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:21.583
So we knew we had to go and see

00:05:21.583 --> 00:05:24.291
where Tesco's waste was being sent

00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:26.333
and we knew the exact
location it was going to.

00:05:26.333 --> 00:05:27.958
So, I took a flight to Poland

00:05:27.958 --> 00:05:30.625
and I teamed up with my colleague Wojciech

00:05:30.625 --> 00:05:34.583
and we simply got in the car
and drove out to Zielona Gora

00:05:34.583 --> 00:05:37.541
and did a drive-by to
look at the facility.

00:05:37.541 --> 00:05:41.458
You can see piles and
piles and piles of plastic.

00:05:41.458 --> 00:05:42.625
This is a exit gate.

00:05:49.708 --> 00:05:51.375
What we found in Zielona Gora

00:05:51.375 --> 00:05:55.250
was a long industrial
building, a warehouse.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.458
It must have been as long as
a football field or longer.

00:05:57.458 --> 00:06:00.416
There were hundreds of tons of material

00:06:00.416 --> 00:06:02.916
just sitting out there waiting
to be sent somewhere else.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:04.916
Mountains of it.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.333
The reason plastic's going there is that

00:06:09.333 --> 00:06:12.125
there's a particular British
run company called Eurokey.

00:06:12.958 --> 00:06:16.666
And Eurokey opened this
large sorting facility

00:06:16.666 --> 00:06:18.791
on the outskirts of Zielona Gora.

00:06:18.791 --> 00:06:21.166
And that's where almost all the waste

00:06:21.166 --> 00:06:24.458
that's coming through this
supermarket system ends up.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:29.875
There are two main reasons why

00:06:29.875 --> 00:06:32.458
a lot of plastic waste is exported.

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:35.250
The first is that disposing of plastic

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.041
in a developed economy like the U.K.,

00:06:37.041 --> 00:06:39.250
or Germany, or U.S. is expensive.

00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:41.750
If you have to landfill, for example,

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.291
if you have to bury the plastic
because you can't use it,

00:06:44.291 --> 00:06:48.625
that costs, let's say 100
pounds a ton to do in the U.K.,

00:06:48.625 --> 00:06:51.500
but the equivalent cost in
Poland is significantly less.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.125
It might cost you 20 or 30 pounds a ton

00:06:54.125 --> 00:06:56.000
to use a landfill facility in Poland.

00:06:58.291 --> 00:07:01.625
But there are also lots
of government incentives

00:07:01.625 --> 00:07:03.375
that are designed to make recycling

00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:05.125
a more attractive proposition.

00:07:05.125 --> 00:07:07.583
You get credits from the government

00:07:07.583 --> 00:07:11.000
for exporting material for recycling.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.541
So if your choice is export to Poland

00:07:14.541 --> 00:07:18.333
or recycle the material in the U.K.,

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:20.541
the government gives you
as much financial support

00:07:20.541 --> 00:07:23.375
for exporting as it does
for doing it domestically.

00:07:27.416 --> 00:07:29.416
I mean the most important exercise

00:07:29.416 --> 00:07:31.000
for the purposes of this
project was to find out

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.166
what happened to the bad plastic,

00:07:32.166 --> 00:07:34.500
because we know that some kinds
of plastic are easy to use.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.458
You know, the problem that
the world has to grapple with

00:07:37.458 --> 00:07:38.791
is what happens to the bad plastic?

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:40.000
What happens to the stuff that

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.375
simply isn't economic to use again.

00:07:44.166 --> 00:07:46.000
So one of my digital trackers

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.166
was inside a lentil puff
wrapper, like a snack wrapper.

00:07:50.166 --> 00:07:52.666
And I was very keen to
see where that one went.

00:07:52.666 --> 00:07:54.833
And I saw it on the move again,

00:07:54.833 --> 00:07:57.958
about 24 hours after it had
been sorted at Zielona Gora,

00:07:57.958 --> 00:07:59.541
and it headed east across Poland

00:07:59.541 --> 00:08:01.375
to a tiny town called Poniatowa.

00:08:02.666 --> 00:08:04.416
And I found out there
that it had been sent

00:08:04.416 --> 00:08:06.041
to a company called Stella Pack.

00:08:07.666 --> 00:08:09.916
Stella Pack are an Eastern
European bag maker,

00:08:09.916 --> 00:08:12.458
they make garbage bags, essentially.

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:15.083
Sort of black sacks and things like that.

00:08:15.083 --> 00:08:16.916
And they have a facility
there that can turn

00:08:16.916 --> 00:08:20.041
soft plastic back into soft plastic.

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:22.166
But they also have a way
to dispose of the plastic

00:08:22.166 --> 00:08:24.875
they can't use, they have
an onsite incinerator.

00:08:24.875 --> 00:08:27.333
Which provides heat and
energy for their factory.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.625
Plastics are made from oil.

00:08:31.625 --> 00:08:34.666
They're essentially fossil
fuels, so they burn really well.

00:08:36.125 --> 00:08:39.125
They burn in a similar
way as crude oil does.

00:08:39.125 --> 00:08:44.125
And so they're great material
for incinerating for energy.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.416
And in Poland they have
a huge cement industry.

00:08:49.416 --> 00:08:52.000
The cement industry requires
a lot of heat and energy

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.000
to calcify limestone powder
and turn it into cement.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.666
They have these gigantic
kilns that run up to

00:08:57.666 --> 00:09:01.083
1,500 degree Celsius
or 2000 degree Celsius.

00:09:02.083 --> 00:09:05.958
And that has traditionally,
relied on Polish coal

00:09:05.958 --> 00:09:09.291
for the energy source to heat the kilns.

00:09:09.291 --> 00:09:12.458
But in recent years, the
cement industry has discovered

00:09:13.416 --> 00:09:16.041
all this waste material
coming into Poland,

00:09:16.041 --> 00:09:18.625
and that there's an abundance of it

00:09:18.625 --> 00:09:20.541
and that it's very, very cheap.

00:09:20.541 --> 00:09:24.250
And so it's widespread
practice across Poland now

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.958
for plastic garbage and
other types of garbage

00:09:26.958 --> 00:09:30.375
to be incinerated for energy
for the cement industry.

00:09:30.375 --> 00:09:31.791
It's actually better than free fuel

00:09:31.791 --> 00:09:33.125
because they are paid to take it.

00:09:33.125 --> 00:09:36.000
If you're a cement maker,
waste brokers in Poland

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.000
will pay you a small sum per
ton to take their waste plastic

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.416
and incinerate it in your kiln.

00:09:42.416 --> 00:09:44.416
So you're actually making
money from your fuel source.

00:09:44.416 --> 00:09:45.791
It's a fantastic deal,

00:09:45.791 --> 00:09:47.750
if you're in the business
of making cement.

00:09:51.083 --> 00:09:55.375
The tracker inside the Tesco
plastic bag stopped pinging

00:09:55.375 --> 00:09:57.875
when it arrived in Zielona
Gora after a few days.

00:09:57.875 --> 00:10:00.166
And I assumed it had been
shredded, or destroyed,

00:10:00.166 --> 00:10:02.041
or trampled on, or taken.

00:10:02.041 --> 00:10:04.000
And then when I got back from Poland,

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.958
I was surprised to see
its location had changed,

00:10:06.958 --> 00:10:10.666
and it gave its location
as southern Turkey.

00:10:10.666 --> 00:10:13.083
A couple of hundred kilometers
from the Syrian border

00:10:13.083 --> 00:10:15.083
in an industrial estate.

00:10:15.083 --> 00:10:17.250
Not all the plastic that goes to Turkey

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.625
is gonna end up being dumped or buried

00:10:19.625 --> 00:10:21.916
or incinerated illegally.

00:10:21.916 --> 00:10:23.708
There are good recycling
companies in Turkey

00:10:23.708 --> 00:10:26.333
that have great reputations,
but unfortunately,

00:10:26.333 --> 00:10:29.625
Turkey as a destination for
waste has huge problems.

00:10:29.625 --> 00:10:32.125
And a lot of the plastic
that goes to Turkey

00:10:32.125 --> 00:10:33.958
ends up being illegally disposed of.

00:10:36.916 --> 00:10:40.000
The tracker ended up at
this industrial estate.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.125
A sort of quite remote one.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:48.083
There was no plastic
recycling company nearby

00:10:48.083 --> 00:10:49.458
that we could identify,

00:10:49.458 --> 00:10:52.250
so we were confused as
to why it was there.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:55.625
And we sent a journalist to the ground,

00:10:56.791 --> 00:10:59.000
to drive out and visit this site.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:03.791
What she found outside a
warehouse, just left in a big pile,

00:11:03.791 --> 00:11:05.250
was tons and tons of waste

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.583
from plastic waste from around Europe.

00:11:08.458 --> 00:11:10.958
The facility itself wasn't
a recycling facility.

00:11:10.958 --> 00:11:12.291
It was obviously just left there

00:11:12.291 --> 00:11:14.291
before it could be moved somewhere else.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:20.125
I don't have any idea what
happened to the tracker material

00:11:20.125 --> 00:11:22.916
that went to that
industrial estate in Turkey.

00:11:22.916 --> 00:11:25.291
I can't imagine that it
was possible to recycle it,

00:11:25.291 --> 00:11:27.458
but I don't know what
happened to it after that.

00:11:27.458 --> 00:11:30.833
But we know that Tesco waste material

00:11:30.833 --> 00:11:35.000
has been found in that very
area for a long period of time.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.416
As recently as a few months ago,

00:11:37.416 --> 00:11:41.833
I spoke to an activist who
found a Tesco carrier bag,

00:11:41.833 --> 00:11:43.291
just left by the side of a field

00:11:43.291 --> 00:11:45.291
in a smoldering pile of burnt plastic.

00:11:45.291 --> 00:11:47.125
Obviously it had been illegally dumped.

00:11:50.458 --> 00:11:53.708
Tesco is a very large supermarket chain.

00:11:53.708 --> 00:11:55.500
It's the UK's largest by far.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.166
It makes an enormous amount
of profit every year.

00:11:58.166 --> 00:12:00.666
And it also has the incredible

00:12:00.666 --> 00:12:02.375
logistics infrastructure in place

00:12:02.375 --> 00:12:04.291
to make sure it's fresh
bread and milk and meat

00:12:04.291 --> 00:12:06.000
at all its stores around the U.K.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.708
It has a network.

00:12:07.708 --> 00:12:11.583
And if this entity can't
properly dispose of plastic,

00:12:11.583 --> 00:12:14.750
what hope is there for a little
cash-strapped town council

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.541
that we want to dispose of
our plastic by the curbside.

00:12:27.791 --> 00:12:30.583
The reason Tesco is
exporting plastic is because

00:12:30.583 --> 00:12:33.541
it's the most cost efficient
solution to their problem.

00:12:34.541 --> 00:12:38.083
So the reality is despite
all the nice words

00:12:38.083 --> 00:12:39.333
and the good intentions,

00:12:39.333 --> 00:12:41.875
that still the vast
majority of soft plastic

00:12:41.875 --> 00:12:44.583
that goes into the market is
either gonna be incinerated

00:12:44.583 --> 00:12:45.875
or it's gonna be buried.

